AU  - P. Sarma
AU  - D. S. Taylor
AU  - M. Moh
AU  - T. -S. Moh
T2  - SoutheastCon 2026
AB  - Google Gemini is a widely used large language model among users involved in computer science, including students, educators, and software developers. An important capability of such models is the generation of visual diagrams, which can help users understand complex computer science concepts such as process scheduling or network protocols. However, Gemini frequently produces syntactically invalid Mermaid code, preventing diagrams from rendering correctly and reducing the usefulness of its responses. This paper presents a lightweight framework designed to improve the syntactic correctness of Mermaid code generated by Gemini. The proposed approach combines retrieval-based guidance, prompt refinement, and iterative correction within an automated wrapper that detects, repairs, and validates invalid diagram code. Experimental results show that the prototype improves the reliability and effectiveness of Gemini's diagram-generation capabilities, increasing its usefulness as a support tool for computer science education and development.
