AB  - Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) supports the efficient development of complex technical systems but requires significant expertise and manual effort. This paper investigates the current state of applying Large Language Models (LLMs) to support system modeling tasks, particularly in dealing with semi-formal languages such as the System Modeling Language (SysML). A structured literature review identifies twenty publications where LLMs are used for system modeling tasks, including the description of the technical set-up, evaluation of the solution and current existing gaps and limitations. Based on the findings, the paper proposes a modular prompting framework for translating SysML v1 to v2 structure diagrams, addressing key syntactic and semantic challenges. The results indicate potential for increased efficiency but highlight the need for evaluation metrics allowing for estimable LLM outputs. Finally, the paper outlines further research directions addressing sufficient and relevant context information as well as evaluation approaches.
