TI  - Infusing Structural Knowledge for Language Generation with Relational Graph Diffusion
AU  - S. Wang
AU  - W. Zhang
AU  - J. Zhu
AU  - Z. Wang
AU  - Y. Xia
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Data Mining Workshops (ICDMW)
AB  - Recent (large) language models have demonstrated their promising performance in general-purpose language generation tasks. However, it is hard to apply them in the scenarios where structural knowledge (e.g., knowledge graph) is provided as context or background information for reasoningbased language generation. Diffusion models have demonstrated its effectiveness in controllable generation due to their flexible architectures. In this paper, we leverage the controllability of diffusion models, and firstly explore the potential reasoning ability of diffusion models to infuse structural knowledge for logicallycontrollable language generation. We first associate the semantic space of texts with the symbolic space of KGs to construct a unified KG-sentence interaction graph. Then we propose a novel relational graph diffusion based on a new structural attention mechanism to iteratively propagate structural knowledge and perform structured reasoning. The resulting framework KGDIFF can generate logical and plausible sequences that encompasses the complex relations of concepts in the KGs. We evaluate our method on two general tasks: knowledge-grounded dialogue generation and generative commonsense reasoning, and a domainspecific task: biomedical question answering. Notably, KGDIFF substantially outperforms previous methods on all three tasks across eight datasets, and is also competitive with recent powerful large language models on domain-specific tasks using much fewer parameters (300M vs 7B).
