TI  - Context Graph: Conversational State Tracking for Knowledge Graph Construction
AU  - S. M. Vinod
AU  - D. Dissanayake
AU  - R. Wilkhu
T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Inventive Computation Technologies (ICICT)
AB  - The volume of new medical research is growing too fast for humans to read and organize manually. To solve this, researchers use Knowledge Graphs (KGs) to turn text into structured data (connecting drugs, diseases, and proteins). However, existing computer programs struggle to read complex medical text because they often process one sentence at a time, forgetting the context. The primary objective of this study is to introduce Context Graph, a novel Conversational State Tracking system that resolves implicit entity references by treating the reading process as a stateful conversation with a Large Language Model (GPT-4o). To ensure data fidelity, we enforce strict structural validation via Pydantic and standardize entities using the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS). Key findings demonstrate that Context Graph achieves a state-of-the-art accuracy of 74.92% on the MINE benchmark, significantly outperforming the previous best method (KGGen at 66.07%). Furthermore, a real-world pilot study on 378 drugs from DrugBank yielded over 130,000 structured relationships, proving the system’s viability for high-density, context-heavy biomedical text extraction.
