AB  - Automation of pharmaceutical laboratory environments face significant obstacles. Those laboratories are characterized as brownfield systems, posing significant challenges for the integration of fully automated solutions due to their heterogeneity, legacy equipment, and human-centered design. As a result, conventional automation approaches, which rely on static programming and tightly integrated systems, are insufficient to cope with the variability and dynamic nature of real-world laboratory workflows. In this work, we present an architecture for robotic task planning and execution based on a Belief–Desire–Intention (BDI) agent implemented using large language models (LLMs). The proposed approach enables non-expert users to specify goals in natural language, which are translated into executable robotic action sequences through an iterative, human-in-the-loop planning process. To enable interaction with robotic systems, the architecture leverages the Model Context Protocol (MCP) as a standardized interface between the LLM-based agent and external tools. Additionally, this contribution compares the dynamic ROS-MCP server, relying on the ROS Bridge, to a custom MCP server relying on rclpy. Presenting a switch from complex syntactic message processing to semantic controlled tools.
