TY  - CHAP
TI  - Digital Twin as a Behavior Model of a Physical System
AU  - Roberto Minerva
AU  - Noel Crespi
T2  - The Foundation for Digital Twins: An Architectural, Technical, and Software Perspective
AB  - <p>It focuses on behavior model as a core element of Digital Twins, capturing how physical systems act in response to stimuli and dynamic events. It presents behavior model as the representation of system actions, interactions, rules, and constraints that govern state changes over time. The chapter discusses the challenges involved in behavior modeling, i.e., the process of designing and construct the model. Behavior modeling serves the needs of progressively capturing the system logic that drives physical changes while also integrating relevant environmental elements and constraints that influence the expected behavior of the physical system. An illustrative example based on state machine is used to clarify several issues in the modeling process. The integration of AI, particularly generative AI, for iteratively refining behavior aspects to be represented in the model is explored. The explainability associated to the existence of a well&#x2010;structured behavior model is also highlighted and discussed. A behavioral simulation model validates system dynamics and enable scenario testing, important for advancing prognosis and prescriptive digital twins that anticipate malfunctions and anticipate or suggest corrective actions. The chapter concludes with a recap of behavior model benefits for creating adaptive, intelligent Digital Twins.</p>
