TI  - A Survey of Knowledge Graph–Based Semantic Representations for Medical Text Analysis
AU  - M. C. Amrani
AU  - M. Mataoui
AU  - K. B. Bey
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Speech, Multimodal and Advanced Communication Systems (ICSMACS)
AB  - Unstructured medical text from clinical notes, guidelines, scientific literature, and clinical trial records contains rich knowledge essential for advancing biomedical research and clinical practice. However, its heterogeneity, scale, and semantic complexity hinder effective integration, interpretation, and reuse. Knowledge Graphs (KGs) provide a semantic representation that normalizes medical concepts, integrates heterogeneous sources, and supports reasoning for accurate retrieval, decision support, and knowledge discovery. This review surveys peer-reviewed studies published between 2020 and 2025 that apply KGs to medical textual data. The literature is organized along three dimensions: (i) KG construction and enhancement methods, including ontology-driven, data-driven, and hybrid approaches; (ii) application domains, spanning biomedical translation, drug repurposing, diagnosis, adverse drug reaction detection, personalized medicine, and general tasks such as recommendation, question answering, information retrieval, and summarization; and (iii) emerging synergies between KGs and Large Language Models (LLMs) for grounded reasoning, retrieval, and generation. We synthesize methodological trends, datasets, and evaluation practices; provide a consolidated comparison of representative works; and highlight persistent challenges related to uncertainty modeling, interoperability, scalability, and privacy. Finally, we discuss promising directions for developing faithful, interoperable, and clinically impactful KG-based systems for medical text.
