TI  - Evaluating the Capabilities of MLLMs to Accurately Extract Information from Complex Technical Standards & Diagrams
AU  - P. Scalise
AU  - N. Bray
AU  - R. Ho
AU  - M. Hempel
AU  - H. Sharif
T2  - 2026 International Wireless Communications and Mobile Computing (IWCMC)
AB  - Multimodal large language models have opened new possibilities for the automated extraction of information from complex technical documents, yet their reliability on technical documents such as dense standards-body specifications, and particularly on the diagrams embedded within them, remains inconsistent. This paper presents an evaluation of 10 models and their respective pipeline configurations, including frontier multimodal models and a class-leading open-source retrieval-augmented generation system (RAGFlow), assessed against 120 weighted questions curated by subject matter experts spanning six telecommunication standards documents of varying complexity. Gemini 3 Pro and GPT-5.2 achieved the highest overall weighted accuracies of 97.1% and 95.6%, respectively, while the best RAGFlow configuration reached 64.1%. Across all models and tiers, figure comprehension emerged as a persistent and universal weakness. Even the top-performing frontier models reached only 87% correctness on figure-based questions. Across the full model population, 42% of figure comprehension responses were scored as entirely incorrect, being the highest failure rate of any content category by a wide margin. In contrast, process and procedure questions achieved 97% full-credit rates across the same population. These results indicate that while general technical text and structured data are now handled reliably by modern MLLMs, accurate extraction of information from block diagrams, state machines, and annotated schematics remains an open and unsolved problem for current generative transformer-based models.
