AU  - H. Kirar
AU  - V. Tiwari
AU  - S. Shukla
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Electrical, Communication, and Computing Technologies (iCONECCT)
AB  - Due to the accelerated growth of the unstructured clinical documentation, an urgent necessity of effective and precise summarization techniques has arisen. The current paper introduces a lightweight, hybrid framework of summarization, which involves extractive and abstractive methods, but optimized to run on a CPU-only architecture. The model does not need annotated datasets and instead works directly on unstructured clinical text, which is why it is suitable in the resource-constrained healthcare environment. The main aspects are entity-conscious extraction, dynamic sentence scoring on the basis of clinical importance, and a quality-controllable generation unit with a slightly altered T5 transformer. A semantic validation layer is used to provide consistency in facts, and fallback mechanisms are provided to deal with errors. The comparison with various clinical corpora demonstrates the suggested system to be superior to baseline extractive and GPT-based models in their performance based on ROUGE, BERTScore, and entity preservation. The architecture can process over 89 documents/hour at standard hardware. The framework provides a practical, deployable framework towards improving clinical decision-making and reporting filling the gap between precision, fluency, and practicality in medical NLP.
